{"explanation":"Strings strings are immutable in JavaScript, so inputs are never mutated. Iterating with the string iterator (Array.from or spread) splits by Unicode code points rather than UTF-16 code units, which keeps surrogate pairs (e.g. emoji) intact. Reversing that array and joining produces the reversed string. Non-string input is coerced with String(); this reverses by code point, not by grapheme cluster, so combining marks will move relative to their base characters.","code":"function solve(s) {
  const str = typeof s === 'string' ? s : String(s);
  return Array.from(str).reverse().join('');
}"}

{"explanation":"Return null for an empty (or non-array) input. Otherwise iterate once with a loop, tracking the largest value, starting from the first element. A loop is used instead of Math.max(...arr) to avoid call-stack limits on very large arrays. The input is only read, never mutated. NaN handling: if any element is NaN, the result is NaN, matching Math.max semantics.","code":"function solve(arr) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr) || arr.length === 0) return null;
  let max = arr[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const v = arr[i];
    if (Number.isNaN(v) || Number.isNaN(max)) {
      max = NaN;
    } else if (v > max) {
      max = v;
    }
  }
  return max;
}"}

{"code":"function solve(arr) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr) || arr.length === 0) return null;
  let max = arr[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
