{"explanation":"Accepts a full URL or a bare query string. It drops the fragment (#...), then takes everything after the first '?'. A string with no '?' is treated as a bare query, unless it contains '://', in which case it is a URL with no query and gives {}. Segments are split on '&' and empty ones are skipped. Each segment is split at the first '=', and a missing '=' gives an empty value. '+' becomes a space before percent-decoding, so a literal plus written as %2B survives. Decoding works on runs of %XX escapes, so UTF-8 multi-byte sequences decode correctly, and a malformed run is left as-is instead of throwing. Values for repeated keys are pushed in order. Own-property checks and Object.defineProperty keep keys like '__proto__' and 'constructor' from touching the prototype. The input is a string and is never mutated. Non-string input returns {}.","code":"function solve(input) {
  const result = {};
  if (typeof input !== 'string') return result;
  let s = input;
  const hash = s.indexOf('#');
  if (hash !== -1) s = s.slice(0, hash);
  const q = s.indexOf('?');
  if (q !== -1) s = s.slice(q + 1);
  else if (s.includes('://')) return result;
  const decode = (t) =>
    t.replace(/\+/g, ' ').replace(/(?:%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+/g, (m) => {
      try {
        return decodeURIComponent(m);
      } catch (e) {
        return m;
      }
    });
  for (const part of s.split('&')) {
    if (part === '') continue;
    const eq = part.indexOf('=');
    const rawKey = eq === -1 ? part : part.slice(0, eq);
    const rawVal = eq === -1 ? '' : part.slice(eq + 1);
    const key = decode(rawKey);
    const val = decode(rawVal);
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(result, key)) {
      result[key].push(val);
    } else {
      Object.defineProperty(result, key, {
        value: [val],
        writable: true,
        enumerable: true,
        configurable: true
      });
    }
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"Use a stack. For each character: push opening brackets '(', '[', '{'; on a closing bracket, pop and verify it matches the corresponding opener, returning false if the stack is empty or mismatched. Ignore all other characters. The string is balanced only if the stack is empty at the end. The input string is never mutated (strings are immutable and only a local stack is modified). Non-string input is coerced via String() for safety.","code":"function solve(s) {
  const str = String(s);
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  const openers = new Set(['(', '[', '{']);
  const stack = [];
  for (const ch of str) {
    if (openers.has(ch)) {
      stack.push(ch);
    } else if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(pairs, ch)) {
      if (stack.length === 0 || stack.pop() !== pairs[ch]) {
        return false;
      }
    }
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}"}

{"explanation":"solve takes an input value and returns a new flat array of its JSON scalars (string, number, boolean, null). It walks the structure with an explicit stack instead of recursion on the call stack, so deeply nested arrays cannot overflow it, while preserving left-to-right order: arrays are pushed in reverse so items pop in original order. The input is only read, never modified, and the result is a fresh array. A non-array input is treated as a single scalar and returned as a one-element array.","code":"function solve(input) {
  const result = [];
  const stack = [input];
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const item = stack.pop();
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      for (let i = item.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        stack.push(item[i]);
      }
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"Use a lower-bound binary search over the sorted array without modifying it. Keep lo=0 and hi=length. While lo<hi, compute mid; if arr[mid] < target, move lo to mid+1, else set hi=mid. This converges on the first index where arr[i] >= target, which is the leftmost occurrence if the target exists. After the loop, return lo if it is in range and arr[lo] === target, else -1. This handles duplicates, empty arrays, and a target outside the array range, and runs in O(log n) time with O(1) extra space.","code":"function solve(arr, target) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) return -1;
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + ((hi - lo) >>> 1);
    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      lo = mid + 1;
    } else {
      hi = mid;
    }
  }
  return lo < arr.length && arr[lo] === target ? lo : -1;
}"}

{"explanation":"Classic dynamic-programming Levenshtein distance using two rolling rows so memory is O(min(m,n)). The shorter string is used for the row width. Row prev[j] holds the distance between the first i-1 chars of a and the first j chars of b; each cell is the minimum of deletion, insertion, and substitution (cost 0 if chars match, else 1). Inputs are only read, never mutated. Handles empty strings: the result is the other string's length.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  if (a === b) return 0;
  if (a.length < b.length) { const t = a; a = b; b = t; }
  const m = a.length, n = b.length;
  if (n === 0) return m;
  let prev = new Array(n + 1);
  let curr = new Array(n + 1);
  for (let j = 0; j <= n; j++) prev[j] = j;
  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    curr[0] = i;
    const ca = a.charCodeAt(i - 1);
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      const cost = ca === b.charCodeAt(j - 1) ? 0 : 1;
      const del = prev[j] + 1;
      const ins = curr[j - 1] + 1;
      const sub = prev[j - 1] + cost;
      let best = del < ins ? del : ins;
      if (sub < best) best = sub;
      curr[j] = best;
    }
    const t = prev; prev = curr; curr = t;
  }
  return prev[n];
}"}

{"explanation":"Take the input as a string, drop any #fragment, and if it is a full URL (a '?' appears before any '='), keep only what follows the first '?'. A leading '?' is also stripped. Split on '&' and skip empty segments. Split each segment at the first '=' only, so values may contain '='. A key with no '=' gets the value ''. Decoding replaces '+' with a space first, so a literal plus written as %2B survives, then applies decodeURIComponent. If that throws on malformed or invalid UTF-8 escapes, the fallback decodes each run of valid %XX escapes separately and leaves bad runs unchanged, so one bad escape never discards the rest. Values go into arrays in order, so duplicates are preserved. Keys are set with Object.defineProperty and looked up with hasOwnProperty, so keys like __proto__ or constructor are stored as ordinary data and do not touch the prototype. The input is a string and is never mutated.","code":"function solve(input) {
  let s = input == null ? '' : String(input);
  const hash = s.indexOf('#');
  if (hash >= 0) s = s.slice(0, hash);
  const q = s.indexOf('?');
  if (q >= 0 && s.slice(0, q).indexOf('=') < 0) s = s.slice(q + 1);

  const decode = (str) => {
    str = str.replace(/\+/g, ' ');
    try {
      return decodeURIComponent(str);
    } catch (e) {
      return str.replace(/(?:%[0-9a-fA-F]{2})+/g, (run) => {
        try {
          return decodeURIComponent(run);
        } catch (e2) {
          return run;
        }
      });
    }
  };

  const result = {};
  if (s === '') return result;

  for (const part of s.split('&')) {
    if (part === '') continue;
    const eq = part.indexOf('=');
    const rawKey = eq >= 0 ? part.slice(0, eq) : part;
    const rawVal = eq >= 0 ? part.slice(eq + 1) : '';
    const key = decode(rawKey);
    const val = decode(rawVal);
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(result, key)) {
      result[key].push(val);
    } else {
      Object.defineProperty(result, key, {
        value: [val],
        writable: true,
        enumerable: true,
        configurable: true
      });
    }
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"code":"function solve(arr) {
