{"explanation":"Use a lower-bound binary search over the sorted array without mutating it. Keep lo=0 and hi=arr.length. While lo<hi, compute mid=lo+((hi-lo)>>>1); if arr[mid]<target, move lo to mid+1, otherwise set hi=mid. This converges on the first index whose value is >= target. After the loop, return lo if it is in range and arr[lo]===target, else -1. This handles duplicates (returns the leftmost), empty arrays, and targets smaller or larger than all elements, in O(log n) time. The signature is solve(arr, target).","code":"function solve(arr, target) {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + ((hi - lo) >>> 1);
    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      lo = mid + 1;
    } else {
      hi = mid;
    }
  }
  return lo < arr.length && arr[lo] === target ? lo : -1;
}"}

{"explanation":"Use BigInt with fast doubling so large n stays exact and runs in O(log n) time. Fast doubling: F(2k)=F(k)*(2*F(k+1)-F(k)), F(2k+1)=F(k)^2+F(k+1)^2. Input n is validated as a non-negative integer (number or bigint); no inputs are mutated. The result is returned as a BigInt for exactness, since Number loses precision past F(78).","code":"function solve(n) {
  if (typeof n === 'bigint') {
    if (n < 0n) throw new RangeError('n must be >= 0');
  } else if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
    throw new RangeError('n must be a non-negative integer');
  }
  const target = BigInt(n);
  function fib(k) {
    if (k === 0n) return [0n, 1n];
    const [a, b] = fib(k >> 1n);
    const c = a * (2n * b - a);
    const d = a * a + b * b;
    return (k & 1n) === 0n ? [c, d] : [d, c + d];
  }
  return fib(target)[0];
}"}

{"explanation":"Uses the iterative Euclidean algorithm on absolute values, so the result is always nonnegative and inputs (primitive numbers) are never mutated. gcd(0,0) returns 0. Works for any safe integers; the loop terminates since the remainder strictly decreases. Local copies a and b are reassigned, not the caller's values.","code":"function solve(x, y) {
  let a = Math.abs(x);
  let b = Math.abs(y);
  while (b !== 0) {
    const t = a % b;
    a = b;
    b = t;
  }
  return a;
}"}

{"explanation":"Classic dynamic programming Levenshtein distance using two rolling rows so memory is O(min(m,n)). The shorter string is used for the row width. Row prev[j] holds the distance between the first i-1 characters of a and the first j characters of b; each cell is the minimum of deletion (prev[j]+1), insertion (curr[j-1]+1), and substitution (prev[j-1] + cost, where cost is 0 if characters match). Inputs are only read, never mutated. Edge cases: an empty string yields the length of the other string; identical strings yield 0.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  if (a === b) return 0;
  if (a.length < b.length) { const t = a; a = b; b = t; }
  const m = a.length, n = b.length;
  if (n === 0) return m;
  let prev = new Array(n + 1);
  let curr = new Array(n + 1);
  for (let j = 0; j <= n; j++) prev[j] = j;
  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    curr[0] = i;
    const ca = a.charCodeAt(i - 1);
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      const cost = ca === b.charCodeAt(j - 1) ? 0 : 1;
      const del = prev[j] + 1;
      const ins = curr[j - 1] + 1;
      const sub = prev[j - 1] + cost;
      let best = del < ins ? del : ins;
      if (sub < best) best = sub;
      curr[j] = best;
    }
    const tmp = prev; prev = curr; curr = tmp;
  }
  return prev[n];
}"}

{"code":"function solve(n) {
  const m = Math.floor(n);
