{"explanation":"Single-pass state machine over the characters of the row. Inside quotes, a doubled quote ("") emits a literal quote and a single quote closes the quoted section; commas are kept as data. Outside quotes, a comma ends the field and a quote opens a quoted section. The final field is pushed at the end, so an empty string yields [''] and a trailing comma yields a trailing empty field. The input string is never mutated and a new array is returned. Non-string input is coerced with String, and null/undefined become an empty string.","code":"function solve(row) {
  const s = row == null ? '' : String(row);
  const fields = [];
  let cur = '';
  let inQuotes = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    const c = s[i];
    if (inQuotes) {
      if (c === '"') {
        if (s[i + 1] === '"') {
          cur += '"';
          i++;
        } else {
          inQuotes = false;
        }
      } else {
        cur += c;
      }
    } else if (c === ',') {
      fields.push(cur);
      cur = '';
    } else if (c === '"') {
      inQuotes = true;
    } else {
      cur += c;
    }
  }
  fields.push(cur);
  return fields;
}"}

{"explanation":"solve(array, size) validates that array is an array and size is a positive integer (Number.isInteger(size) && size > 0), throwing a TypeError/RangeError otherwise. It then builds a new result array by iterating with index i stepping by size and pushing array.slice(i, i + size). slice returns shallow copies, so the input is never mutated, and the final chunk holds any remainder. An empty array yields an empty result.","code":"function solve(array, size) {
  if (!Array.isArray(array)) {
    throw new TypeError('First argument must be an array');
  }
  if (!Number.isInteger(size) || size <= 0) {
    throw new RangeError('Chunk size must be a positive integer');
  }
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i += size) {
    result.push(array.slice(i, i + size));
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"The function first checks that the input is a string and matches /^[0-9]+$/ (nonempty, ASCII digits only), returning false otherwise. It then iterates from the rightmost digit to the left, doubling every second digit (subtracting 9 if the result exceeds 9), and sums all digits. The input is valid if the sum is divisible by 10. Nothing is mutated; only local variables are used.","code":"function solve(input) {
  if (typeof input !== 'string' || !/^[0-9]+$/.test(input)) {
    return false;
  }
  let sum = 0;
  let double = false;
  for (let i = input.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    let d = input.charCodeAt(i) - 48;
    if (double) {
      d *= 2;
      if (d > 9) d -= 9;
    }
    sum += d;
    double = !double;
  }
  return sum % 10 === 0;
}"}

