}"}

{"explanation":"solve takes an array (possibly nested) of JSON scalars and returns a new flat array. It uses an explicit stack-based iterative traversal instead of recursion in the call stack, so deeply nested input cannot overflow the stack, while still processing nested arrays at every depth. The input is never mutated: the stack holds iterators/indices over the original arrays and only reads them, and results are pushed to a fresh output array. Order is preserved (depth-first, left to right). Non-array values (strings, numbers, booleans, null) are appended as-is. Sparse array holes are skipped via an 'in' check, matching Array.prototype.flat behavior. Non-array top-level input is returned wrapped in a single-element array.","code":"function solve(input) {
  if (!Array.isArray(input)) return [input];
  const out = [];
  const stack = [{ arr: input, i: 0 }];
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const frame = stack[stack.length - 1];
    if (frame.i >= frame.arr.length) {
      stack.pop();
      continue;
    }
    const idx = frame.i++;
    if (!(idx in frame.arr)) continue;
    const item = frame.arr[idx];
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      stack.push({ arr: item, i: 0 });
    } else {
      out.push(item);
    }
  }
  return out;
}"}

{"explanation":"Copy the input pairs so nothing is mutated, then sort the copies by start (ties broken by end). Sweep once, keeping the current merged interval. If the next start is <= the current end, the intervals overlap or touch at an endpoint, so extend the end to the max of the two. Otherwise push the current interval and start a new one. Output pairs are fresh arrays. Empty or non-array input returns []. Touching means a shared endpoint, such as [1,2] and [2,3]; [1,2] and [3,4] stay separate.","code":"function solve(intervals) {
  if (!Array.isArray(intervals) || intervals.length === 0) return [];
  const sorted = intervals
    .map(function (iv) { return [iv[0], iv[1]]; })
    .sort(function (a, b) { return a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]; });
  const result = [];
  let cur = sorted[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {
    const next = sorted[i];
    if (next[0] <= cur[1]) {
      if (next[1] > cur[1]) cur[1] = next[1];
    } else {
      result.push(cur);
      cur = next;
    }
  }
  result.push(cur);
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"solve takes a value and returns a new string with HTML special characters escaped. It coerces the input to a string (null/undefined become an empty string) and does a single-pass regex replace using a lookup map, so '&' is not double-escaped and the input is never mutated (strings are immutable anyway).","code":"function solve(input) {
  const map = {
    '&': '&amp;',
    '<': '&lt;',
    '>': '&gt;',
    '"': '&quot;',
    "'": '&#39;'
  };
