{"explanation":"Use a null-prototype object as the accumulator so keys like __proto__ and constructor are stored as ordinary own properties rather than hitting Object.prototype. Assignment via obj[key] on a null-prototype object is safe for __proto__ because there is no inherited __proto__ setter. Existence is checked with Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call to avoid inherited lookups. The input array is only read, never mutated. Non-string elements are skipped since only string occurrences are counted. Note that JSON.stringify of the result works normally, but JSON.parse of a string containing '__proto__' creates an own property too, so the output remains faithful; a null-prototype object is returned, which serializes identically to a plain object.","code":"function solve(arr) {
  const counts = Object.create(null);
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) return counts;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    if (typeof item !== 'string') continue;
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(counts, item)) {
      counts[item] += 1;
    } else {
      Object.defineProperty(counts, item, {
        value: 1,
        writable: true,
        enumerable: true,
        configurable: true
      });
    }
  }
  return counts;
}"}

{"explanation":"solve(arr, size) validates that arr is an array and size is a positive integer (Number.isInteger(size) && size > 0), throwing a TypeError/RangeError otherwise. It then builds a new array of chunks using slice in a loop stepping by size, so the input is never mutated and the last chunk holds the remainder. An empty array yields an empty result.","code":"function solve(arr, size) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
