{"explanation":"The function first checks that the input is a string and matches /^[0-9]+$/ (nonempty, ASCII digits only), returning false otherwise. It then iterates from the rightmost digit to the left, doubling every second digit (subtracting 9 if the result exceeds 9), and sums all digits. The input is valid if the sum is divisible by 10. Strings are immutable and no input is modified.","code":"function solve(input) {
  if (typeof input !== 'string' || !/^[0-9]+$/.test(input)) {
    return false;
  }
  let sum = 0;
  let double = false;
  for (let i = input.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    let d = input.charCodeAt(i) - 48;
    if (double) {
      d *= 2;
      if (d > 9) d -= 9;
    }
    sum += d;
    double = !double;
  }
  return sum % 10 === 0;
}"}

{"explanation":"Strings are immutable in JS and the function only reads its inputs, so nothing is mutated. For each string, iterate over its characters, keep only ASCII letters (A-Z, a-z) by checking char codes, lowercase them, and count occurrences in a 26-slot array. Two strings are anagrams if their count arrays are identical. Non-letters (digits, spaces, punctuation) are ignored. Two strings with no letters both yield all-zero counts and are treated as anagrams (empty multisets are equal). Non-string inputs are coerced via String() for robustness.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  function counts(s) {
    const c = new Array(26).fill(0);
    const str = String(s);
    for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
      let code = str.charCodeAt(i);
      if (code >= 65 && code <= 90) code += 32;
      if (code >= 97 && code <= 122) c[code - 97]++;
    }
    return c;
  }
  const ca = counts(a);
  const cb = counts(b);
  for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
    if (ca[i] !== cb[i]) return false;
  }
  return true;
}"}

{"explanation":"Use the Sieve of Eratosthenes. Coerce n to an integer with Math.floor; if it is not a finite number or is < 2, return an empty array. Create a Uint8Array of size n+1 (marks composites), then for each i from 2 while i*i <= n, if i is unmarked, mark multiples starting at i*i in steps of i. Finally collect all unmarked indices >= 2 in increasing order. The input is a primitive number, so nothing is mutated, and a new array is returned each call.","code":"function solve(n) {
  n = Math.floor(Number(n));
  if (!Number.isFinite(n) || n < 2) return [];
  const composite = new Uint8Array(n + 1);
  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (!composite[i]) {
      for (let j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
        composite[j] = 1;
      }
    }
  }
  const primes = [];
  for (let k = 2; k <= n; k++) {
    if (!composite[k]) primes.push(k);
  }
  return primes;
}"}

