{"explanation":"Return a new array without mutating the input. For an empty array, return an empty copy. Normalize k with ((k % n) + n) % n so negative k (left rotation) and |k| >= n both work. Then the result is the last r elements followed by the first n - r elements, via slice. Non-integer k is truncated with Math.trunc; non-finite k is treated as 0.","code":"function solve(arr, k) {
  const n = arr.length;
  if (n === 0) return [];
  let step = Number.isFinite(k) ? Math.trunc(k) : 0;
  const r = ((step % n) + n) % n;
  if (r === 0) return arr.slice();
  return arr.slice(n - r).concat(arr.slice(0, n - r));
}"}

{"explanation":"Uses the iterative Euclidean algorithm on absolute values so the result is always nonnegative. Inputs are primitives, so nothing is mutated. gcd(0,0) returns 0, and gcd(a,0) returns |a|. Numbers are used as-is; for BigInt inputs the same logic works since it detects the type and uses the matching zero.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  const isBig = typeof a === 'bigint' || typeof b === 'bigint';
  let x = isBig ? BigInt(a) : Number(a);
  let y = isBig ? BigInt(b) : Number(b);
  const zero = isBig ? 0n : 0;
  if (x < zero) x = -x;
  if (y < zero) y = -y;
  while (y !== zero) {
    const t = x % y;
    x = y;
    y = t;
  }
  return x;
}"}

